# Data Pipeline

Run `python pipeline.py`. The script scrapes five paginated catalogue pages with `requests` + `BeautifulSoup`, visits each product detail page to obtain its category, cleans and converts the data, creates normalized SQLite tables, loads them, executes five SQL queries, and validates the JOIN with pandas.

The required fixed rate is **1 GBP = 105.50 INR**. No live currency lookup is used.

Parsing failures for numeric fields are median-imputed after successful parsing. Title/category failures cause a row to be dropped because those fields are essential to identify and group a catalog item. Availability is parsed as `True` when its text contains `In stock`.

The five SQL queries collectively demonstrate SELECT/WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT, DISTINCT, BETWEEN/IN and a PK/FK JOIN. Their query strings and outputs are saved in `sql_outputs.md`. `books.db` is regenerated from scratch, so it is not necessary to depend on a prebuilt database.

## SQL artifact
The script writes `sql_outputs.md` after a successful live scrape. Because this build environment cannot resolve the public scraping host, that runtime artifact is intentionally not fabricated.
